{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1515512-kaboom","id":1515512,"title":"Kaboom!","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-18T22:28:33-07:00","id":1515512,"title":"Kaboom!","content":"Rereading RotSE.<br /> <br /> So say the Scarlet Empress blows Thorns or Halta off the face of Creation with the Realm Defence Grid, or whatever city is most important to your PCs like Nexus, Whitewall, maybe even Lookshy.<br /> <br /> Obviously this is a super-atrocity, but what exactly happens in the aftermath when it comes to the Underworld? Does the entire darn thing just suddenly, spectacularly drop into place in the realm of darkness, shoving other things out of the way? A sudden, perfect replica of the city with hundreds of thousands of dead guys in it?<br /> <br /> What's the immediate aftermath? Do the Deathlords approach them? Manacle and Coin enslave half of the population? How does Stygia deal with the thousands and thousands of sudden new dead guys? How do the dead guys in Halta or whatever themselves react? I mean obviously the place suddenly becomes the biggest Shadowland this side of ... whatever, assuming it wasn't already that way. Who jumps in to take advantage of the situation while the attempted Reclaimation is happening 'up on the surface'? Do the Neverborn themselves react to this sudden megadeath?<br /> <br /> Ideas, people! Give me your ideas!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2023-tsukai","name":"Tsukai","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-06-19T23:11:28-07:00","id":1515627,"title":"","content":"Which edition's Underworld are you using? Until Abyssal comes out we won't have a lot of detail, but everything I've seen for 3rd edition is that they are making changes to the geography and metaphysics of the Underworld. Personally, I prefer the old version of the Underworld that is mostly a mirror of Creation. <br /> <br /> Based on my recollections of 1st edition Underworld (and probably with a fair bit of head canon mixed in), I think the Underworld is a bit morphic based on the memories and expectations of the spirits that reside in any given spot. So if you go into the Underworld around any major city, you would find a city of the dead there too. But it would be &quot;bigger on the inside&quot;. Large sections of it may look fairly similar to the actual city in Creation, with familiar architecture and possibly even street layouts, but when you turn a corner or go down certain alleys you'll find yourself in an older version of the city, from hundreds of years ago, or the time of the Contagion, or even the First Age. It all depends on the ghosts who live in that neighborhood and what they remember or expect from their time alive. But over time most of the ghosts eventually tire of unlife, lose their fetters, or have some misfortune that they can't reform from and enter Lethe, so the most plentiful dead are more recent, and older dead get increasingly uncommon - except for the Contagion dead, of which there are still many. So the cities in my Underworld are divided up into layers or regions from various points in time, with the last century or the Contagion era being the largest and most populous, but other eras represented in decreasing size and population as you go further back in history. But some of the oldest and most powerful ghosts live in those ancient areas... And which &quot;layer&quot; you're in can change in unexpected ways as you move around the city.<br /> <br /> So in my game, a city-destroying weapon would probably create a shadowland. During the day, the ruins of the city are there with the most recent shades. Living mortals who come to the shadowland could interact in that terrain during the day, but only the dead could shift away from that into older echos/layers. At night, when the shadowland fully crosses over into the Underworld, both the living and the dead can move out of the recently destroyed echo of the city and into the older versions from before the destruction. Turn a corner and the ruined building before you shimmers back into its original appearance as you enter a neighborhood based on the city from a generation ago, or a completely different building from hundreds of years ago if you go to an older slice of the city's past.<br /> <br /> Culturally, it would be a pretty seismic shift in the local politics of the dead. A huge influx of the recent dead will upset the balance of power. The oldest spirits will include some of the strongest; perhaps the Underworld city is ruled by a thousand year old ghost and her entourage, and they have been able to maintain their influence due to their experience and power until now, but suddenly the population of ghosts swells with a sudden influx of angry/fearful/confused ghosts. Playing to find out what happens next could be really interesting.<br /> <br /> Anyway, that's how it would go in my head canon. Interesting question!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26886-lindharin","name":"Lindharin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"33"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}